<p>US officials told Latin American defence leaders meeting at US Southern Command in Doral, Florida, that military power—not the courts—is the only effective way to dismantle drug cartels, a clearer shift in Washington’s approach under President Donald Trump. White House homeland security adviser Stephen Miller said there is “not a criminal justice solution” to cartels, and he argued the meeting’s military focus reflects that view. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth urged governments to take a more aggressive posture, warning that the Trump administration could act on its own if partners do not confront criminal organizations the US says threaten its security and borders. The message comes as the US steps up activity in the region, including recent actions tied to Mexico and Venezuela cited by officials and reporting. The initiative also stirred political and legal friction. Legal experts and US Democrats have questioned the legality of treating drug traffickers like terrorist groups, and some longtime partners appear uneasy—Colombia did not send a delegation to the conference, according to reporting.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/PAEsOsmyciF-fJwyBJgcKkA-uZaXReU0tEikvtgrdvM53L7Dd-R5WMQku5z2-8f856qXD92joKgmOL0CX0zdWGHH843RSjn38BFsWNvRx_D6iaGqzj1wNz1uaWYhYQBOsBNe8kQnP1avvploHLw2n4NMuthSuMUkr8fizFGmJ4PwYkqshuJdqZ-h4_zlZgkjXzMiirBnlYziKJ2VqnT3gk07KZbeYPL2q4z1RZU8tafm3LqwsBtORmXJRnIgf0d0f0balVhWZZ-ZwKs66w' alt='White House Deputy Chief of Staff for Policy Stephen Miller speaks during the inaugural Americas Counter Cartel Conference at US Southern Command in Doral, Florida, on March 5, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Migration rhetoric: At the gathering, Hegseth framed the region’s challenge as staying Western and Christian, rather than being torn apart by “uncontrolled mass migration” and other threats.</li><li>Partner unease: Reporting described scepticism that more military force will stop drug gangs, and placed Hegseth’s push in a broader context of rising US intervention, including a US ground attack in Venezuela that ended with Nicolás Maduro’s capture.</li></ul><blockquote>We have learned after decades of effort that there is not a criminal justice solution to the cartel problem. - Stephen Miller</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Stephen Miller (White House): He said cartels can only be defeated with military power and argued that decades of effort show the courts cannot solve the problem. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978949/military-force-is-the-only-way-to-defeat-drug-cartels-us'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Pete Hegseth (Pentagon): He urged partners to take a tougher approach and warned the Trump administration could act alone if governments do not confront groups the US says threaten border security. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/watch-hegseth-miller-urge-latin-american-allies-to-be-more-aggressive-against-drug-cartels'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>US critics (legal experts, Democrats): They questioned the legality of the strategy, including treating drug traffickers like terrorist organizations such as Al Qaeda and Islamic State, according to reporting. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-stephen-miller-cartels-military-latin-america-mexico-venezuela-pete/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Colombia (government): Colombia did not send a delegation to the conference, which reporting cited as a sign that some traditional US partners feel unsettled by the US approach. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978949/military-force-is-the-only-way-to-defeat-drug-cartels-us'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>The Guardian (reporting/critics): It reported dismay and scepticism that more military force will stop drug gangs, and described the broader context as escalating US intervention in the region. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/05/hegseth-latin-america-drug-cartels'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978949/military-force-is-the-only-way-to-defeat-drug-cartels-us'>Military force is the only way to defeat drug cartels: US</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-stephen-miller-cartels-military-latin-america-mexico-venezuela-pete/'>U.S. tells Latin American defence leaders that military force is only way to defeat cartels</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-urges-latin-america-to-use-military-force-against-drug-cartels/a-76237257'>US urges Latin America to use military force against drug cartels</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/watch-hegseth-miller-urge-latin-american-allies-to-be-more-aggressive-against-drug-cartels'>WATCH: Hegseth, Miller urge Latin American allies to be more aggressive against drug cartels</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/5/trump-administration-doubles-down-on-military-action-in-latin-america'>Trump administration doubles down on military action in Latin America</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/05/hegseth-latin-america-drug-cartels'>Dismay as Hegseth urges Latin American allies to join ‘offense’ against cartels</a> - theguardian.com</li></ul>